          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"“I love you” (literally, in the surrounding emphatic construction, “so surely do I love you”).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The speaker directly and emphatically presents his present love for Maria as true, in answer to her doubt about whether his change of heart is genuine and lasting.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"elsker jeg Dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The focal expression avows love but does not itself undertake an action, obligation, or defined relationship. The same turn contains a separate statement about living and dying with Maria, but its commitment force should not be transferred to the focal words.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"elsker jeg Dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Du er den, jeg vil dø med, men først vil\\njeg leve meget, meget længe med Dig.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"Although the reunion is intensely emotional, the expression is represented as a deliberate, reasoned answer to Maria rather than a reflex-like discharge produced by affect.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg svarer: »Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.82,\"reason\":\"In addition to avowing love, the expression functions as emphatic reassurance and confirmation: it directly answers Maria’s fear that his present feeling is imagined or will be regretted tomorrow. This reassuring function is secondary to the dominant avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"For det er jo sandt, ikke noget, Du imorgen fortryder, ikke\\nnoget, Du nu bilder Dig ind\"},{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"elsker jeg Dig\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At their reunion, Maria embraces the speaker and asks whether his apparent change is genuine rather than a temporary response to missing his lover. He answers with an oath-like comparison culminating in the focal avowal.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression itself contains no explicit undertaking. Elsewhere in the same answer, the speaker says that Maria is the one with whom he will die and, first, live for a very long time; this is commitment-laden but is a separate statement and is not formally phrased as a promise. Maria’s immediate uptake is belief and happiness: the narration says she understands that he speaks truth. The later supplied passage depicts their marriage, but that does not alter the focal expression’s primarily avowal-and-reassurance force.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not independently recognise the work from prior familiarity.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence is a literary representation mediated by the speaker-narrator. It strongly represents sincere affection at this moment, including the statements that he no longer doubts his own love and"
